  "content": {
    "id": "p29-q11-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "11",
    "prompt": "Suppose a bag contains $b$ black balls and $w$ white balls. We randomly pick a ball. If the ball is white, then we put two white balls back into the bag. If the ball is black, then we put two black balls back into the bag. Then we randomly pick another ball from the bag. If the ball is white, then we put two white balls back into the bag. If the ball is black, then we put two black balls back into the bag. This continues indefinitely. Find the probability that:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "the first pick is black",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{b}{b+w}$",
          "worked": "$$P(B_1)=\\frac{b}{b+w}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 9
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "the second pick is black",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{b}{b+w}$",
          "worked": "Each pick removes one ball and returns two of its colour, increasing the total by one.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(B_2)&=\\frac{b}{b+w}\\times\\frac{b+1}{b+w+1}+\\frac{w}{b+w}\\times\\frac{b}{b+w+1}\\\\P(B_2)&=\\frac{b(b+1)+wb}{(b+w)(b+w+1)}\\\\P(B_2)&=\\frac{b(b+w+1)}{(b+w)(b+w+1)}\\\\P(B_2)&=\\frac{b}{b+w}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p29-q11-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "the $n$th pick is black.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\frac{b}{b+w}$.\n\nSequences with the same counts of black and white picks have equal probability, so every position has the same marginal probability.",
          "worked": "For a particular sequence of $n$ picks containing $r$ black picks and $n-r$ white picks, multiplying its successive conditional probabilities gives\n\n$$P(\\text{sequence})=\\frac{\\displaystyle\\prod_{i=0}^{r-1}(b+i)\\prod_{j=0}^{n-r-1}(w+j)}{\\displaystyle\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(b+w+k)}.$$\n\nAn empty product is $1$. This probability depends only on the counts of each colour, not their order. Swapping the first and $n$th positions pairs sequences with a black first pick with equally probable sequences with a black $n$th pick. Therefore,\n\n$$\\begin{align*}P(B_n)&=P(B_1)\\\\P(B_n)&=\\frac{b}{b+w}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 48
      }
    ]
  },
